# systemd unit for the vike-gatewayhub GATEWAY daemon — the one process that owns every connection to
# every roster venue (docs/decisions/0164-one-daemon-owns-every-venue-connection-vike-gatewayhub.md;
# the design is docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-13-vike-gatewayhub-design.md), in the
# ONE-PROJECT-FOLDER shape. See docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md for the runbook: the install order, the
# rollback and what to read in the journal.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IT IS, AND WHAT IT IS TO THE OTHER UNITS. Wave 1 of that record moved every venue call the
# data daemon makes into this process: the market-data and recorder feeds, the REST of backfill and
# the chart seed, the venue catalog, the Deribit options poll and the OANDA practice-token history
# read. WAVE 2 MOVES THE TRADING DAEMON'S VENUE HALF IN TOO: the order, cancel and modify path, the
# user-data (fill) streams, the reconcile reads, the arming table and the live-account locks of every
# roster venue now run HERE, one account session per (venue, account), and the trading daemon
# (deploy/vike-tradehub.service) reaches them over the loopback `trade` wire as a CLIENT that signs
# nothing and opens no venue order socket. The data daemon (deploy/vike-datahub.service) is the
# other client: it keeps the store, the recorder, the md hub and every served verb, and its strategy
# lane (decision 0164, D8) is how the trading daemon's strategy feeds arrive. This daemon still holds NO
# store and commits nothing; what it signs it signs for a tradehub that asked, and it cancels
# nothing on its own (D6: a lost trade link cancels no resting order). The backtesthub never talks
# to this one. Because it now holds the live positions' sessions, a BREACH or a crash here is a
# trading event, not a gap in a tape: see MemoryMax= and Restart= below and the runbook's
# "restart drills" (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md).
#
# ⚠ ORDER, AND WHY IT IS `Wants=` + `After=` AND NEVER `Requires=`. The data daemon's unit AND the
# trading daemon's unit order themselves after this one and WANT it, so a box that starts them starts
# this one first and, at shutdown, stops them first (the reverse of `After=`): the trading daemon's
# graceful stop runs its resting-order cancel sweep THROUGH this daemon, so this daemon must still be
# serving while the tradehub tears down and must go down AFTER it. Neither REQUIRES it: a restart of
# this daemon must not take them down (the tradehub's trade link re-opens and re-teaches the adapter
# at the new epoch, the datahub's remote clients retry, and a recorder stopped with it would open a
# gap in the tape for nothing). ⚠ `After=` orders a START or a STOP that systemd plans in ONE
# transaction (boot, shutdown, `systemctl stop a b`); it does NOT make `systemctl restart
# vike-gatewayhub` wait for the tradehub, which is why the runbook's per-step drill stops the
# tradehub FIRST. The converse of the order is the trap this file's runbook is written around — a
# data daemon started with this one absent has no venue access at all, and a tradehub started with
# it absent waits a bounded time and then runs its demo/live accounts as NOT RUNNING
# `GatewayUnavailable` (never as paper, decision 0156) — so install and enable THIS unit BEFORE the
# release that contains the remote clients reaches the box (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md, "Install
# order").
#
# ⚠ EVERY PATH BELOW IS THE PLACEHOLDER PROJECT ROOT /srv/vike-<unit>. It is an example, not a
# requirement — the daemon works from ANY absolute path, and THE ROOT IS THE PARAMETER THIS FILE IS
# SUBSTITUTED FOR. On a box that already runs the data and trading daemons the root is THEIR project
# folder: one folder, one `settings/`, each daemon with its own write grant. Choose your own by ONE
# rule: a single directory on a disk with room, owned by the user the service runs as, holding bin/
# and settings/ (with state/ and state/logs/). Then substitute it everywhere in the installed copy and
# check nothing was missed:
#
#   R=/your/project/root
#   sed "s,/srv/vike-<unit>,$R,g" deploy/vike-gatewayhub.service > /tmp/vike-gatewayhub.service
#   grep -n /srv/vike-<unit> /tmp/vike-gatewayhub.service    # no output means every placeholder went
#   sudo install -Dm644 /tmp/vike-gatewayhub.service /etc/systemd/system/vike-gatewayhub.service
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now vike-gatewayhub
#
# A SYSTEM install also wants `User=`/`Group=` added and `WantedBy=` changed to `multi-user.target`
# (this file ships the `--user`-friendly `default.target`). ⚠ Read the hardening block at the bottom
# first: under a --user manager the sandbox directives are applied as NO-OPS on a stock Ubuntu box —
# measured, docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md.
#
# ⚠ ONE UNIT PER DAEMON: a daemon has ONE unit file, named without a suffix, and the project root is a
# substitutable parameter rather than a second file. WHAT A BOX SUBSTITUTES AND ADDS, as for every unit
# in this directory: the root; `User=`/`Group=`, deliberately NOT shipped here because this file is
# published to the public source mirror, whose redaction rewrites a box's user name into a phrase that
# is not a legal systemd user name; `WantedBy=multi-user.target` for a SYSTEM install; and, where the
# box wants a failure pager, `OnFailure=vike-notify@%n.service` inline OR deploy/attach-onfailure.sh's
# drop-in — never both, `OnFailure=` is a LIST directive and the notifier would fire twice.
#
# ⚠ THE INSTALLED COPY OF A SIBLING UNIT IS A HAND-DERIVED ARTIFACT, NOT A RENDERING OF ITS TEMPLATE.
# MEASURED on the live box 2026-10-13: the installed data unit names no `--store` argument (the store
# is its `config.store_root` row) and grants the box's `data` directory where the tracked template
# grants `market_data`. Do not diff this file against a sibling's installed copy and read the
# difference as a defect; a unit on that box is installed by hand from the tracked file and drifts only
# by hand (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md, "The installed units differ from their templates").
#
# The shape is MACHINE-CHECKED: crates/vike-ops/tests/container_deploy/deploy_layout_gate.rs reads every
# unit in this directory and fails if one names a second location, spreads its write grant over more
# than one ReadWritePaths= LINE, grants a path that covers a binary it runs or its own settings
# directory, sets VIKE_LOG_DIR, points EnvironmentFile= at the credential store, or runs an
# ExecStartPre= binary from outside the project folder (or behind a `-`, which would make the
# pre-check non-fatal).

[Unit]
Description=vike-gatewayhub — gateway daemon: the one process that owns every venue connection, the data daemon's reads and the trading daemon's orders alike
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target
# The IBKR DEMO gateways, BOTH products: the IB Gateway (`deploy/vike-ibkr-gateway.service`) and the
# Client Portal Gateway's JVM and login (`deploy/vike-ibkr-cpapi.service`,
# `deploy/vike-ibkr-cpapi-login.service`). The IBKR session is a VENUE connection, so its owner is
# this daemon now, and these two lines MOVED HERE from `deploy/vike-tradehub.service` (decision 0140:
# a box rides ONE IBKR session and each unit's `ExecCondition=` asks the settings database which, so
# exactly one pair's units start and the other's are skipped). This daemon starts once the started
# ones are up (the API port answers / the session is authenticated), so IBKR mounts against a
# logged-in gateway. `Wants=`, not `Requires=`: a skipped, failed or absent unit leaves this daemon
# starting as before, with IBKR NOT RUNNING for want of its gateway. A unit that does not exist is
# ignored by both lines.
#
# ⚠ DURING THE SOAK THE SAME LINES STAY ON `deploy/vike-tradehub.service` TOO, so a rollback needs
# no unit edit: the previous tradehub opens its own IBKR session and must find the gateway up. A
# restart of this daemon leaves a RUNNING IBKR unit alone, and the lines are deleted from the
# tradehub's file only after the soak (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md, "Wave 2 release runbook").
#
# ⚠ THE COST, STATED: on a COLD START (boot, or `systemctl start` with the IB Gateway stopped) this
# daemon's start waits for `deploy/ibkr-gateway/wait-gateway.sh` (the API port answers; 180 s at
# most, the unit's `TimeoutStartSec=240`) because `After=` orders it behind the gateway's start job,
# and the data and trading daemons, which are ordered after THIS one, wait behind it in turn. A
# failed IB Gateway start (login refused, no display) releases them at its failure, not before. A
# plain restart of this daemon, which is what a deploy does, waits for nothing WHILE the IB Gateway
# unit is active. ⚠ It is NOT so during IBKR's daily logoff: the IB Gateway unit drops at 23:45:00 UTC
# and is back at about 23:46:25 on the 2026-10-12 and 13 evidence (a 60 s `RestartSec=` and its login),
# and `Wants=`+`After=` put that unit's start job INTO a restart of this one, so a restart (a deploy,
# a hand drill) that lands in the window blocks behind it, and a failed IB Gateway start holds it
# for up to the 180 s wait. With a live tradehub that is longer than the 120 s dead-man grace. Do not
# deploy or restart this daemon between 23:40 and 23:55 UTC (the runbook says so at each step).
Wants=vike-ibkr-gateway.service vike-ibkr-cpapi.service vike-ibkr-cpapi-login.service
After=vike-ibkr-gateway.service vike-ibkr-cpapi.service vike-ibkr-cpapi-login.service

[Service]
Type=simple

# ⚠ **THE RELEASE SHIPS ONE BACKEND BINARY, `vike-backend`, AND `gatewayhub` IS A TOOL INSIDE IT** —
# the same multicall deploy/vike-datahub.service runs as `datahub`; the two units differ only in the
# verb. Dispatch is on a SUBCOMMAND, so there is no symlink here and no `203/EXEC` class to inherit
# from a dangling one. The binary and the verb are ONE edit, never two: `crates/vike/src/lib.rs`'s
# `resolve` matches a tool by EXACT string with no aliases, so `bin/vike-backend vike-gatewayhub` is
# exit 2 with a tool list — a daemon that "started" and did nothing, not a crash.
# The standalone crate binary (`vike-gatewayhub`) exists too and is what the crate's own tests spawn;
# nothing on a box installs it. Building the shipped one yourself:
#   cargo build --release -p vike-backend --bin vike-backend --features full
#
# ⚠ THE BINARY AND THIS FILE REACH THE BOX SEPARATELY, AND NOTHING SEQUENCES THEM.
# `deploy/sbin/vike-trader-ci-deploy` replaces the BINARY, unattended, on a tag; THIS file only reaches
# the box when an operator runs the `install -Dm644` recipe above and `systemctl daemon-reload`, and
# that helper restarts this unit only if the INSTALLED copy of the helper itself names it (it is
# installed by hand too: docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md, "Install order", step 1). The daemon takes NO
# flags: everything it can be told is a settings row, and a typo on this line is refused with exit 2
# rather than ignored.
#
# WorkingDirectory IS the project root: the settings walk and the rolling log resolve off it with no
# extra variable (crates/vike-paths/src/state_path.rs's `project_state_dir`).
WorkingDirectory=/srv/vike-<unit>
ExecStart=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-backend gatewayhub

# --- PRE-FLIGHT: refuse a broken configuration instead of starting degraded ----------------------
# The same check deploy/vike-datahub.service and deploy/vike-tradehub.service run, for the same
# reason: `vike-cli config check` resolves the SAME settings directory through the SAME loader this
# daemon uses and makes the EXIT CODE the product, so a non-zero status fails the unit BEFORE
# ExecStart runs. docs/decisions/0013-degrade-vs-refuse.md is the rule it implements. The daemon's
# own boot (`crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/gatewayhub_cli/boot.rs`'s `boot_root`) loads settings too, so
# on a box with this line a broken tree is met here, once, rather than as a restart loop.
#
# ⚠ Deliberately NOT `--strict` (it promotes a 0644 credential-store permission finding into a
# refusal the workspace argues must never be one), and NO leading `-` (that would make a failed
# pre-check non-fatal, i.e. delete this line while appearing to have it).
# ⚠ It needs a second binary in bin/, or systemd fails the unit with status=203/EXEC:
#   cargo build --release -p vike-cli --bin vike-cli
#   install -m755 target/release/vike-cli /srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-cli
ExecStartPre=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-cli config check

# --- the listen address --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The listener binds the `config.gatewayhub_addr` row of this box's settings database, and
# loopback port 7883 when the row is absent — which is what this template relies on. A non-loopback
# address is REFUSED at startup with exit 2, and there is NO flag, no key and no row that lifts it:
# the daemon holds every venue connection and its handshake is plaintext, so it serves this host only
# (`crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/gatewayhub_cli/boot.rs`'s `resolve_bind` is the rule). Reach it from
# elsewhere through an SSH tunnel to its loopback port. Do not restate the bind as a property in
# `Description=` — see
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/container_deploy/deploy_layout_gate/descriptions_and_hardening.rs`'s
# `no_unit_description_claims_what_an_environment_file_can_falsify`.
# ⚠ The row is also what the DATA DAEMON dials. Move one and the other with it, or every venue call
# fails at its first connect, naming the address it tried.
#
# ⚠ **NO KEYS, NO START.** This daemon holds venue credentials, so unlike a key-less data daemon it
# has no unauthenticated mode: with no gateway hub key in the credential store it logs why and exits.
# Mint the keys BEFORE enabling this unit (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md, "Install order", step 2):
# `vike-cli gatewayhub setup` mints the observe/control pair AND the order-scope Trade key the
# tradehub signs its trade lease with, and `vike-cli datahub setup --strategy-lane` mints the
# datahub's strategy-lane key. All of them are ROWS of the one `vike.db` hub-key table: no key lives in
# this unit, in an environment file or on a command line. WITHOUT the Trade key this daemon serves
# its data lane and NO trade surface (the tradehub then reports its demo/live accounts NOT RUNNING
# `GatewayUnavailable`, naming the key).

# --- WHERE SETTINGS AND CREDENTIALS LIVE ---------------------------------------------------------
# `<project>/settings/` holds the settings database and `state/`. No settings file is read (decision
# 0086) and no setting is read from the process environment (decision 0111): a retired variable in
# this unit REFUSES startup, naming the row that replaced it (`vike_config::REMOVED_ENV`). The binary
# finds the project by walking UP from its working directory; the line below makes the answer
# EXPLICIT and independent of WorkingDirectory — set it and no walk happens at all.
#
# ⚠ A DEPLOYED box has a BINARY, not a source checkout, so the directory must exist, AND its `state/`
# sub-directory (and `state/logs/`), which under ProtectSystem=strict the daemon cannot create for
# itself:
#   install -d -m700 /srv/vike-<unit>/settings /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/logs
#
# ⚠⚠ **ONE DAEMON READS THE VENUE CREDENTIALS, AND THIS IS IT** (decision 0164, verdict 6). It reads
# them by SCOPED reads — its hub keys, one OANDA practice-token field of one account per history
# request, and (wave 2) each trade account's own credential book when its lease opens — never the
# whole venue map at boot, and a value reaches no log line, no reply and no `Debug`. The venue
# settings rows (`venue.<name>.*`) are READS too.
#
# ⚠ **THE `account` ROWS, THE FLAGS, THE VENUE SETTINGS AND THE DEPLOYMENT NAMES ARE RE-READ AT EACH
# TRADEHUB STEP, NOT AT THIS DAEMON'S START** (the trade lane's `RefreshedTrade`: the arming table, a
# `TradeOpen`, an adapter start and the preflight's plan each read the store afresh). Activating an
# account, changing its tier or rotating its key therefore reaches the venue half at the next tradehub
# restart, with no restart of THIS daemon; an adapter that is already running keeps the credentials it
# started with. A restart of THIS daemon is needed only for what the boot reads: the hub keys, the
# listen address and the log level.
Environment=VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings

# ⚠ NO `EnvironmentFile=`, and that is the point rather than an omission. The directive is a second
# lever on the process environment that WINS over every `Environment=` line here (measured, both
# orders), and on a shared root it reaches the OTHER daemons' operator file: one stray line there
# would arrive in the one process that holds every venue key. Everything this daemon is told is a
# settings ROW; the console's `RUST_LOG` is the one developer switch and is not a unit concern.

# --- turn the trace FILE log DOWN ----------------------------------------------------------------
# RUST_LOG and the log_level row filter the CONSOLE only; the file layer defaults to `trace`. The file
# level is the `preferences.log_file_level` ROW (no line in this file sets it); write it once, as the
# service user with this unit's VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR, before the first start:
#   vike-cli config set preferences.log_file_level warn
# ⚠ Deliberately NO VIKE_LOG_DIR. Unset, the project layer resolves the rolling log to
# <project>/settings/state/logs — inside the `settings/state` path of the write grant at the bottom of
# this file, shared with the data daemon's log directory under a prefix of its own (`vike-gatewayhub`), so the two do not
# prune each other's files. Watch it with `du -sh /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/logs`.

# --- keep the daemon from starving the box -------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ THE CAP IS A SUM OF SEVEN TERMS, and the unit's life is longer than wave 1: this daemon holds every
# fetch ANSWER whole (a lane returns the full `Vec` of rows and the daemon pages it out of memory), and
# it is the process wave 2 puts the Dukascopy JVM, the FXCM native SDK and every account's trade
# session in. A child process and a loaded library both count against THIS cgroup.
#
#   steady  MEASURED on the live box 2026-10-13 (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md carries the table): the
#           data daemon's client-owning half is 45 threads, 6 established venue sockets and a resident
#           set of about 0.5 GB, 1.0 GB at its high-water. That is the WHOLE data daemon, store and
#           page cache included, so it over-states the half that moved here; the stream queues add at
#           most their ceilings (`crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/server/limits.rs`'s
#           `STREAM_PLANE_MEMORY_CEILING_BYTES`, 768 MiB = 48 sessions x the 16 MiB `WIRE_QUEUE_BYTES`,
#           and the options feeds' 8 x 4 MiB). 1.0 GB + 0.75 GB + 0.03 GB = about 1.8 GB.
#   jvm     WAVE 2, and NOT MEASURED: wave 1 starts no JVM.
#           `crates/bridges/dukascopy/src/exec.rs`'s `jvm_args` launches `java -Duser.home=... -Xmx512m
#           -jar <bridge jar>` (`MAX_HEAP_FLAG`; no `-Xms`): the heap is CAPPED at 512 MiB whatever this
#           unit's cgroup says. Without the flag the JVM's default was a quarter of the memory it
#           believes it has: a JDK 17 that finds this unit's cgroup limit sizes it from MemoryMax (a
#           quarter of 8G is 2 GB of heap); one that does not (a `--user` manager, a hidden
#           /sys/fs/cgroup) sizes it from the box (a quarter of 125 GB), which no MemoryMax could hold.
#           512 MiB is exactly the default under the trading daemon's own `MemoryMax=2G`
#           (deploy/vike-tradehub.service), the unit whose execution path can launch it today.
#           The heap is only half the process: metaspace, the code cache, thread stacks and the
#           GC's tables add a few hundred MB. Planned: 0.5 GB heap + 0.5 GB the rest = 1.0 GB for
#           the ONE sidecar; the heap half is now a bound, the rest (`-Xmx` bounds only the heap)
#           is NOT MEASURED.
#           Measure with the sidecar under this unit (`systemd-cgtop`, or `VmHWM` of the java pid)
#           before trusting it.
#   fxcm    WAVE 2. The ForexConnect SDK (`libForexConnect.so` behind `libfcshim.so`) lives IN the
#           process (decision 0164, "Measured on the live box"), in native memory the Rust allocator
#           never sees. Its footprint is not measured separately; the one bound there is: the trading
#           daemon hosts it today (its FXCM demo account is active and armed) inside cgroup peaks of 88
#           to 205 MB per run for ALL its venue sessions (`t0_baselines.md` section 5). 0.25 GB.
#   trade   WAVE 2, DERIVED FROM THE QUEUE BOUNDS AND NOT MEASURED. One account session holds, at their
#           ceilings (`crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/server/limits.rs`): the replay ring
#           (`GW_TRADE_RING_EVENTS`, 20,000 events), the command queue (`GW_TRADE_COMMAND_QUEUE`, 4,096),
#           the bridge-to-forwarder channel (`GW_TRADE_EVENT_CHANNEL`, 4,096) and the control queue
#           (`GW_TRADE_CONTROL_QUEUE`, 1,024). At about 0.5 KiB an event or order and 4 KiB a control
#           frame that is 10 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 18 MiB per account. The count is the SESSION CEILING this file
#           declares in the `GATE-READS trade_sessions ceiling=` line below (18: the box's account rows on
#           2026-10-14, every one of which may run at once but IBKR's two, which exclude each other under
#           decision 0140), so 18 x 18 MiB = 324 MiB; `GW_MAX_TRADE_LEASES` (64) bounds it at 64 x 18 MiB
#           = 1.15 GB. Round the box's figure to 0.35 GB. ⚠ The ring is bounded in
#           EVENTS and not in bytes: one event may be a whole frame (the 64 KiB skip bound), so nothing
#           here PROVES 20,000 of them fit, only that real events are hundreds of bytes.
#   exec    WAVE 2. The 14 bridges' exec halves (REST clients, user-data sockets, signers) were the
#           trading daemon's and are THIS cgroup's now. The only measurement of their size is that
#           daemon's cgroup peak with all of them mounted, 88 to 205 MB per run (the number the fxcm
#           term above also leans on), so it is counted again, in full: 0.25 GB. (The tradehub keeps
#           its core, journal and feeds; none of that comes here.)
#   answer  ONE kline request is ONE batch: `backfill_kline_source` asks the source once for the whole
#           window the caller names, and nothing in the datahub bounds that window, so the largest
#           answer is the largest window an operator asks for. A `Bar` is 120 B, a funding point 32 B,
#           a `QuoteTick` 72 B plus its symbol (`crates/vike-gatewayhub-client/src/proto_tests.rs`'s
#           `the_row_sizes_the_unit_memory_arithmetic_uses` measures them). Ten million bars (19 years of
#           1m, or about four months of 1s) is 1.2 GB, held ONCE: the pager makes one page at a time
#           (it used to build a second copy of the whole answer, so the peak was twice that; measured on
#           the lane, 3 million bars: 345 MB of rows, +343 MB to split them eagerly, +3 MB lazily). A tick
#           window is capped at 31 days (`GW_TICKS_MAX_SPAN_MS`); its quote count is not measured here, and
#           at several million quotes of about 100 B each it is the same order. The chunked lanes (a day of
#           ticks, a day of OANDA bars) are far under it.
#
#   1.8 GB steady + 1.0 GB jvm + 0.25 GB fxcm + 0.35 GB trade + 0.25 GB exec = 3.65 GB that is there
#   whatever is being fetched. Wave 1's 8G left 4.95 GB over its three fixed terms, FOUR answers of
#   1.2 GB, and 8G was the data daemon's cap, where these same buffers lived until the venue calls moved
#   here, so a request that fitted there fits here. Keeping that promise on top of the two new terms
#   needs 3.65 GB + 4 x 1.2 GB = 8.45 GB; the next whole step up is 10G, which leaves 6.35 GB = FIVE answers
#   (3.65 GB + 6.0 GB = 9.65 GB) and 0.35 GB of slack for the two terms that are not measured (the JVM's
#   non-heap half and the ring's byte size). So the cap is 10G, the one number the wave moves. It is a
#   BACKSTOP and not a promise: `MAX_FETCHES` (32) fetches at the worst window would not fit.
#
# THE LINE BELOW IS READ BY A GATE, which is what makes this comment and the cap one claim: the terms
# above, in MB, and the answer count the cap must hold. `wave2_units.rs`'s
# `the_gatewayhub_memory_cap_covers_the_terms_its_comment_declares` sums them and requires
# `MemoryMax=` to cover the sum, so editing a term here without the cap (or the cap without the term)
# goes red; its own floors (the wave-1 promise of four 1.2 GB answers) stop a term being lowered to
# make a smaller cap pass.
# GATE-READS memory_mb steady=1800 jvm=1000 fxcm=250 trade=350 exec=250 answer=1200 answers_held=4
#
# ⚠ WHAT A BREACH DOES, AND IT IS A TRADING EVENT NOW. A MemoryMax breach is an OOM kill inside THIS
# unit's cgroup, and it takes the whole unit down: the kernel picks a victim by badness inside the
# cgroup (`OOMScoreAdjust=` only reorders the candidates; it cannot exempt the daemon, and it would
# make the JVM child the likelier victim), and systemd's default `OOMPolicy=stop` then stops the rest.
# Every stream session ends, every client reconnects after `Restart=` below, a half-open JForex login
# is lost with it, and EVERY account's venue session drops at once: an order in flight is UNKNOWN to the
# tradehub (the lease answers NEVER-SENT versus UNKNOWN, decision 0164), resting orders stay at the
# venue (D6: nothing is cancelled on a link loss), fills missed in the gap are reconcile's job, and a
# venue in `link_deadman_default` (binance, aster, bybit, okx, polymarket) trips the tradehub's dead-man
# if the daemon is not back inside its grace (120 s). So the cap is where a request too large for the
# box ends, not a way to spare the other sessions, and a history request this big is a reason to ask
# the datahub for a smaller window, never to raise the cap again.
# The old 2G was sized from the steady set alone. Tune to the box's headroom (125 GB on the CI box).
# ⚠ A cgroup limit edited into the INSTALLED copy reaches the RUNNING daemon at `systemctl daemon-reload`,
# not at its next restart (this one is a relaxation, 8G to 10G, so applying it early is harmless); the
# runbook puts the reload in the same sitting as the deploy for that reason.
MemoryMax=10G
# Restart on a crash (an OOM kill included), but not on a clean exit / operator stop.
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5

# --- stop ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `systemctl stop` IS a graceful stop. SIGTERM raises the same stop flag the daemon's whole run order
# is built around (`crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/gatewayhub_cli.rs`'s `run_with` arms the handlers
# before anything can block) and the accept loop drains: every connection is told, a stream session
# writes its `Bye`, a running fetch is cancelled and the connection threads are joined. The decision,
# the measurements and the options that were refused are in docs/ops/graceful-stop.md.
# There is deliberately NO ExecStop=: the daemon stops on the signal, so a stop command could only add
# a SECOND stop route to a design whose whole invariant is that there is one.
KillSignal=SIGTERM
# ⚠ THE BUDGET IS DERIVED, and `crates/vike-ops/tests/container_deploy/graceful_stop_pin.rs`'s
# `the_gatewayhub_unit_outlasts_its_stop_drain` reads it out of THIS file. The first bounded wait in the
# teardown is `crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/server/accept.rs`'s `serve`, which joins the connection
# threads for at most `crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/server/limits.rs`'s `STOP_DRAIN` (half of it politely,
# then the sockets are cut: every lease is told `Bye(ServerStopping)`) and returns whatever has not
# finished: a thread still inside a venue call is left behind and ends with the process.
#
# WAVE 2 ADDS THE SECOND: right after `serve`'s drain it retires the trade sessions
# (`crates/vike-gatewayhub/src/trade/session.rs`'s `TradeHub::shutdown`), ONE SESSION AFTER ANOTHER,
# and each retire queues a stop behind the account's commands and joins the exec thread, whose body is
# the bridge's `begin_detach` then `detach`: a flag raise and a wind-down that waits for the
# user-data pump's next idle check, about one second (`ExecutionClient::begin_detach`'s doc in
# `crates/vike-exec/src/execution_engine/client.rs`; [inferred], not measured at a venue). In the
# PLANNED order the tradehub has already detached every lease at its own stop (its final `Detach`
# retires each adapter on the lease's own thread, in parallel), so this loop finds nothing to do; it
# earns its place in the UNPLANNED one, which is also the deploy's: `vike-trader-ci-deploy` restarts
# THIS unit while the running tradehub still holds all of its leases, and every one of them is retired
# here, serially. The count is SESSIONS, one per `(venue, account)`, and not active accounts: an
# account that does not arm (Dukascopy's two today, an inactive row) holds no session and costs nothing,
# while a row the rollout activates adds one. The ceiling this file declares is 18, the box's account
# rows on 2026-10-14 (the planned rollout reaches 15 sessions with Dukascopy blocked and 17 with it
# unblocked; IBKR's two rows exclude each other, decision 0140, so 18 is one above the real maximum).
# The previous value was sized for 14 and was EXACTLY FULL at the rollout's 15 (5 + 15 + 5 + 5 = 30), so
# the first release after the last live tier would have been SIGKILLed mid-retire under a live tradehub.
# The retire also waits for each lease to say goodbye (`TradeHub::shutdown`, at most 3 s), but every
# lease is told to stop BEFORE any wait begins, so those waits overlap and cost about 3 s once, not
# 3 s a session; that overlap is read from the code and is not measured.
#
# Nothing here is buffered and nothing resting at a venue is cancelled (D6: that is the tradehub's
# own sweep) — the daemon holds no store and commits nothing — so a SIGKILL costs the venues a dropped
# connection where a logoff was owed (IBKR, cTrader, the JForex sidecar), the clients a reconnect and
# no data. THE ARITHMETIC, every term read by the gate except the two estimates it states:
#   STOP_DRAIN 5 s + 18 sessions x 1 s + 5 s for what runs after (the lane drop, the exit) + 5 s margin
#   = 33 s, so 35.
# THE LINE BELOW IS READ BY A GATE: `graceful_stop_pin.rs`'s `the_gatewayhub_unit_outlasts_its_stop_drain`
# takes the session ceiling from it (and refuses one above `GW_MAX_TRADE_LEASES`, or below the box's
# account rows its own constant states), so this comment, the cap on sessions in the code and the number
# on the next line are one claim. Raise the drain or add an account row and raise this too.
# GATE-READS trade_sessions ceiling=18
# The ORDER against the tradehub is not a number: that unit's `After=` this one is what makes systemd
# stop it FIRST, and the tradehub's own `TimeoutStopSec=` is its own budget (deploy/vike-tradehub.service).
TimeoutStopSec=35

# --- the sandbox ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The daemon needs no privileges, makes outbound connections to the venues and one loopback listener.
NoNewPrivileges=true
# ⚠ ProtectSystem=strict mounts the WHOLE filesystem read-only except /dev, /proc, /sys and whatever
# ReadWritePaths= names. READS are unaffected, so the settings and the credential store load normally.
#
# ⚠ MEASURED on the live box (systemd 255, Ubuntu 24.04): under a `systemd --user` manager with the
# distribution default `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1`, these directives are applied
# as NO-OPS — no warning, no failed unit, and `systemctl --user show` still reports them as
# configured. They enforce under the SYSTEM manager. docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md has the probe.
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
PrivateTmp=true
# TWO grants, both SUB-directories, ON ONE LINE: wave 2 makes this daemon write in two places beyond
# its rolling log, and each is the narrowest path that holds the write. It still never gets the
# project root, `bin/` (the binary it executes) or the settings directory itself (the credential
# store), which `deploy_layout_gate` refuses, and the tradehub unit carries the same two paths.
#
#   [1] `settings/state`  (the log directory `settings/state/logs` is inside it, so the wave-1 line is
#       NOT lost). What the trade lane writes there, all of it small data and none of it code:
#         * `LIVE-<route key>.lock`, claimed at a live account's trade lease (the B11 sentinel; the
#           tradehub claims none of its own any more, and a lock it cannot create REFUSES the live
#           lease loudly — `trade/state_dir.rs` says "fail closed");
#         * `gateway-id`, the project token the tradehub compares;
#         * the parked account confirmation (`vike_secrets::account_confirmation::park`), which an
#           un-sandboxed `vike-cli` folds into the store;
#         * the Dukascopy sidecar's JForex home (`state/jforex`).
#       The HALT sentinel in this directory is READ, never written, by this daemon (its dispatch checks
#       it before a submit); the operator's `touch` is the tradehub-side grant's argument and is
#       unchanged (deploy/vike-tradehub.service).
#       ⚠ KNOWN TRADE-OFF, ACCEPTED AND NOT FIXED HERE: a directory grant makes `settings/state/HALT`
#       writable, and writable means DELETABLE, by the daemon the sentinel is meant to stop. This unit's
#       sibling, deploy/vike-datahub.service, calls the identical grant a safety finding for a recorder
#       and refuses it; the difference is that THIS daemon needs the directory for its lock files and
#       `gateway-id` (a lock it cannot create refuses every live lease), and the tradehub already carries
#       the same grant, so the exposure is one more process and not a new class. Nothing in this crate's
#       source removes a file outside its tests (the lock files are claimed with `flock` and left in place),
#       so the risk is a defect or a compromise, which the ceilings do not bound either (decision 0068).
#       The narrower layout would put the locks, `gateway-id` and the JForex home in ONE state
#       subdirectory (a `state/live/`) and grant only that plus `settings/state/logs`; it costs a path
#       change in `vike_boot::live_lock` and the tradehub's own grant, and it is the reopener for this
#       paragraph (docs/ops/gatewayhub-the CI box.md, "Wave 2 release runbook", doubts).
#   [2] `settings/db`  (the settings database). The ONE credential write this daemon owns is cTrader's
#       rotated access/refresh pair (`vike_ctrader::token_store`'s `persist`): the venue spends the old
#       refresh token on use, so a process that does not write the new pair back strands the account
#       on a spent one. SQLite's journal files live beside the database, so the DIRECTORY has to be
#       writable, and that is the same trade the trading daemon's unit argues at length in its own
#       `[2]` block: a filesystem grant cannot see a table, the ceilings are rows in the same file, and
#       decision 0068 is the answer (a ceiling bounds MISTAKES, not a compromised daemon, which holds
#       the keys it bounds). `crates/vike-ops/tests/settings_secrets/credential_writer_gate.rs`'s
#       `WRITER_CALLERS` is the list of code paths that may write there at all. Nothing here DELETES,
#       MOVES or REWRITES the store wholesale.
#
# ⚠ **THE GRANT GROWS HERE, IN THIS FILE, NEVER IN A DROP-IN.** This line grew in the change that made
# this daemon claim a live-account lock and persist a rotated cTrader pair, with the argument above.
# A grant that exists only in an `/etc/systemd/system/*.d/` file is a grant no review of this
# repository ever saw, and this box's owner has ruled drop-ins out. Both paths must EXIST (the install
# recipe above creates `settings/state`; `settings/db` is where the store already lives): under the
# SYSTEM manager systemd refuses a unit whose `ReadWritePaths=` names a missing path (status=226), and
# that refusal is the point.
# ⚠ ONE LINE: deploy_layout_gate.rs fails a unit that spreads its grant over more than one
# ReadWritePaths= line, because a second line is where a grant nobody re-read gets added. NOT the
# project root either: that would cover bin/ (the binary this unit executes) and settings/ (the
# credential store), which the same gate refuses.
ReadWritePaths=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/db


# --- syscall / capability hardening, MEASURED ON THE REAL BINARIES -------------------------------
# The tradehub's whole block, VERBATIM, except one directive. MEASURED on the CI box 2026-08-17, systemd
# 255, with throwaway SYSTEM units running these exact binaries — every line survived a run, the TLS
# handshakes and WebSocket dials included. The set is the one every daemon unit in this directory
# carries; `crates/vike-ops/tests/container_deploy/deploy_layout_gate/descriptions_and_hardening.rs`'s
# `REQUIRED_HARDENING` is the minimum and its `every_daemon_unit_keeps_its_syscall_and_capability_hardening`
# the rule.
#
# ⚠⚠ **`MemoryDenyWriteExecute=` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, AND IT IS THE ONLY DIRECTIVE THIS UNIT OMITS.**
# MEASURED on the live box 2026-10-13 (decision 0164, "Measured on the live box"): the JForex JRE
# (Temurin 17.0.19) REFUSES TO START under `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes`, printing
# "Failed to mark memory page as executable", under the JIT and `-Xint` alike, and starts with it off
# and the rest of the sandbox unchanged. deploy/vike-ibkr-gateway.service already runs without it for
# the same JIT reason (its header says so). This daemon is where the Dukascopy JVM sidecar is
# spawned (wave 2: the trade lane's Dukascopy adapter launches it), so the sandbox of the process that
# owns it cannot carry the directive. THE EXEMPTION IS UNCHANGED BY W17 and so is its argument; what
# wave 2 changes is only that the process it exempts now also holds the trading sessions, which is a
# reason to keep every OTHER directive and to keep the exemption declared, not to widen it.
# ⚠ Wave 1 STARTED NO JVM — its Dukascopy fetch is the keyless `.bi5` archive read — so the directive
# could stay ON until wave 2 and cost nothing; the owner chose to omit it then (record 0164, verdict
# 7(c)), so the unit does not have to change twice. ⚠ NOT a loophole to copy: every other daemon unit
# keeps it, `deploy_layout_gate`'s `every_daemon_unit_keeps_memory_deny_write_execute_unless_declared`
# holds that and names this unit as the one declared exemption, with this reason. The record's
# reopener: Dukascopy reachable without a JVM, or a need to restore the directive — the JVM then
# gets its own unit.
#
# ⚠ PrivateNetwork= is deliberately ABSENT and always will be — this daemon IS a network client.
# `systemd-analyze` charges for that and for holding AF_INET sockets at all; both are unrecoverable
# for anything that talks to a venue. RestrictAddressFamilies= therefore ALLOWS the three families in
# use rather than pretending the internet is optional.
#
# ⚠ The one line with a real blast radius is UMask=0077: new files become owner-only. A future reader
# under a DIFFERENT account breaks on it, loudly, with a permission error rather than silence. That
# is the intended trade for a tree holding venue keys.
CapabilityBoundingSet=
AmbientCapabilities=
RestrictNamespaces=yes
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_UNIX
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
RestrictRealtime=yes
LockPersonality=yes
SystemCallArchitectures=native
SystemCallFilter=@system-service
SystemCallFilter=~@privileged @resources @obsolete @mount @module @reboot @swap @raw-io @debug @clock @cpu-emulation
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectKernelLogs=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
ProtectClock=yes
ProtectHostname=yes
ProtectProc=invisible
ProcSubset=pid
UMask=0077

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
